## 2. Priority 1 — quick wins

### 2.1 `client_max_body_size` — ✅ DONE 2026-09-01

**Applied.** `client_max_body_size 0;` added at the `server` level of
`orthanc-cors-proxy.conf`, validated with `nginx -t`, applied with a graceful
`nginx -s reload`. Inode preserved (bind mount intact).

**Verified** with a POST to a nonexistent path, which creates nothing:

| Probe | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2 MB POST via `orthanc-cors-proxy:8043` | 413 | **404** (forwarded to Orthanc) |
| 2 MB POST via `https://viewer.hospital.lan` | 413 | **404** |
| 20 MB POST via `https://viewer.hospital.lan` | — | **404** |
| `GET /dicom-web/studies?limit=1` (regression) | 200 | **200** `application/dicom+json` |

**Correction to an earlier assumption:** Nginx Proxy Manager was *not* a second ceiling.
It sets `client_max_body_size 2000m;` in its own `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`. The 413 initially
observed through NPM was the CORS proxy's 413 being relayed back through it. **No NPM change
was needed.**

**Still to confirm by actually using it:** save a segmentation brush stroke from a
workstation. The transport limit is proven lifted; whether OHIF's STOW-RS write succeeds
end to end is a separate assertion that only a real save can settle.

**ROLLBACK:** `cat "backup files/orthanc-cors-proxy.conf.bak-20260901-104751" > orthanc-cors-proxy.conf`
then `docker exec orthanc-cors-proxy nginx -t && docker exec orthanc-cors-proxy nginx -s reload`

